# A template of a script: the fixture harness runs it stepped
# (`fixture-test`; docs/dev-practices/headless-mode.md, "How the gate runs a
# fixture"), with its setup (`Editor.Test.Fixture.Setup`): `tree/` is copied
# into the file fake's model under `/tmp/working-set.stepped0`, which
# `@DIR@` stands for, a path of the model with the byte length of the
# `mktemp` template the gate made it with (`/tmp/working-set.XXXXXXXX`),
# since a scene prints byte offsets over it; and git, if asked, answers as
# outside a repository, as the real git does under `/tmp`. The run is
# against the config in `../config/`, of which it loads nothing: there is
# no `reload`, so every command its keys run is one the editor ships. It
# must print exactly `expected` once the directory is written `@DIR@`. It
# writes no file: the headless mode has no state directory here, so the
# working set is kept in memory alone.
#
# It is a working set's range held again when its file is reopened: the
# tree is a workspace by its marker `.project`, so the file opened in it
# is attached to that workspace; a line added to a working set is a range
# held in the file's buffer; closing the buffer detaches the range, and
# opening the file again attaches it, so the set opened shows the line, not
# a drifted quote. It pins what the files feature's open and the buffers
# feature's close hand the working set, whatever carries it.

# `:e` opens the file; the load and the workspace's find are waited for.
key : Semicolon shift
commit e @DIR@/tree/notes.txt
key <Enter> Enter -
join
key j KeyJ -
scene

# `working-set-add` with no name asks for one; `todo` is a new set, and the
# cursor's row, the second, is its range. The echo area says what was
# added; the first add also says the sets are kept in memory alone.
run working-set-add
join
commit todo
key <Enter> Enter -
join
scene

# `close-buffer` closes the file's buffer, and `:e` opens the file again.
run close-buffer
join
key : Semicolon shift
commit e @DIR@/tree/notes.txt
key <Enter> Enter -
join
scene

# `working-set-open` asks for the set, and shows its buffer: the range is
# held in the reopened file, so its line is an excerpt, and none is
# drifted.
run working-set-open
join
commit todo
key <Enter> Enter -
join
scene
